replace_section("## 5.6 Analysis Across Tasks", """
## 5.6 Analysis Across Tasks

The screening task was never chosen on evidence — the Pittsburgh corpus has healthy controls on picture description only — and the first attempt to put evidence behind the deployed four-task battery is reported with its own correction. The pre-registered battery test found a five-task battery at 0.6455 [0.5763, 0.7154] against picture description alone at 0.5687 [0.4998, 0.6393] on the same 288 participants, paired difference +0.0769 [0.0015, 0.1505], graded BATTERY-BETTER. That grade does not survive two design defects, both this project's own, found after the first output was seen and disclosed as such: the participant labels were taken from the first row in file order, which is not the first visit, so for the 18 of 288 participants whose label changes across visits the arm used an arbitrary visit's label; and the battery arm carried 320 features against the single task's 64, so task count was confounded with column count. Section 5.25 redoes the comparison with both defects removed, and the battery gain does not survive (+0.0102 [−0.0579, +0.0818], BATTERY-NOT-CONFIRMED). The two results are not a contradiction: feature concatenation lets a model learn to down-weight a task that carries no signal and equal-weight averaging cannot, so together they localise the apparent battery gain — not in administering more tasks but in being able to weight them — and the cheapest way to down-weight the weak task is not to administer it, because the weak task is the picture. The sentence this once licensed, that the four-task design "can now be defended as a measurement rather than a preference", is withdrawn and reproduced here so that a reader of an earlier draft can see exactly what changed.
""")

